/* Microbiology 9–10: Mycology, applied microbiology */
Z("micro9",0,"m",["Fungi are eukaryotic organisms classified by shape (yeasts, yeast-like, moulds, dimorphic) and by the site of infection (superficial, cutaneous, subcutaneous, systemic, opportunistic). Lab diagnosis uses KOH mount, special stains, culture on Sabouraud agar, antigen tests and serology.","Fungi come in three outfits: single round cells (yeasts), long branching threads (moulds), or both depending on temperature (dimorphic — 'mould in the cold, yeast in the heat').",["Morphological: yeasts (Cryptococcus), yeast-like (Candida — pseudohyphae), moulds (Aspergillus, dermatophytes), dimorphic (Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Sporothrix, Talaromyces).","Clinical: superficial (pityriasis versicolor), cutaneous (dermatophytes), subcutaneous (mycetoma, sporotrichosis, chromoblastomycosis), systemic (histoplasmosis), opportunistic (candidiasis, aspergillosis, mucormycosis, cryptococcosis, PCP).","Lab: 10–20% KOH mount, India ink (Cryptococcus), Gram, PAS, GMS, calcofluor white; culture on SDA (with chloramphenicol, cycloheximide) at 25 °C and 37 °C.","Slide culture/LPCB mount for identification; antigen (galactomannan, β-D-glucan, CrAg), PCR, serology; skin tests."],"Dimorphic: 'Mould in the Cold, Yeast in the Heat'."],
"Fungi are eukaryotic, heterotrophic organisms with rigid cell walls containing chitin and ergosterol in membranes. Morphological classification: yeasts (unicellular, reproduce by budding — Cryptococcus neoformans); yeast-like fungi (form pseudohyphae — Candida); moulds or filamentous fungi (hyphae forming mycelium; septate — Aspergillus, dermatophytes, Penicillium; aseptate — Mucorales); dimorphic fungi (mould at 25 °C/environment and yeast at 37 °C/tissue — Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Coccidioides (spherules), Paracoccidioides, Sporothrix, Talaromyces marneffei). Taxonomic classification by sexual spores: Zygomycota (Mucorales), Ascomycota, Basidiomycota (Cryptococcus), Deuteromycetes (fungi imperfecti). Clinical (systemic) classification: superficial mycoses (pityriasis versicolor — Malassezia; tinea nigra; piedra), cutaneous mycoses (dermatophytoses, cutaneous candidiasis), subcutaneous mycoses (mycetoma, sporotrichosis, chromoblastomycosis, rhinosporidiosis, phaeohyphomycosis), systemic/endemic mycoses (histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis, paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis) and opportunistic mycoses (candidiasis, cryptococcosis, aspergillosis, mucormycosis, pneumocystosis). Laboratory diagnosis: specimens — skin scrapings, nail clippings, hair (dermatophytes), pus, sputum, BAL, CSF, blood, biopsy; direct microscopy — 10–40% KOH wet mount (dissolves keratin; hyphae, spores), calcofluor white with fluorescence, India ink/nigrosin (capsule of Cryptococcus), Gram stain (Candida), Giemsa (Histoplasma); histopathology — PAS, Grocott methenamine silver, mucicarmine (Cryptococcus), H&E; culture — Sabouraud dextrose agar (with chloramphenicol and cycloheximide; incubated at 25 °C and 37 °C for up to 4 weeks), brain heart infusion agar, CHROMagar Candida, bird seed (niger seed) agar (brown colonies of Cryptococcus); identification — colony morphology, lactophenol cotton blue mount, slide culture, germ tube test (C. albicans), sugar assimilation, urease, MALDI-TOF; antigen detection — cryptococcal antigen (latex, LFA), galactomannan (Aspergillus), β-D-glucan (pan-fungal except Mucorales and Cryptococcus), Histoplasma antigen in urine; antibody detection (immunodiffusion, CFT); molecular — PCR, sequencing; skin tests (histoplasmin); antifungal susceptibility testing.",
[["Morphological classification",[["Type","Features","Examples"],["Yeasts","Unicellular, budding","Cryptococcus"],["Yeast-like","Budding + pseudohyphae","Candida"],["Moulds","Hyphae, mycelium","Aspergillus, dermatophytes, Mucor"],["Dimorphic","Mould 25 °C, yeast 37 °C","Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Sporothrix, Talaromyces"]]],
["Clinical classification",[["Category","Examples"],["Superficial","Pityriasis versicolor, tinea nigra, piedra"],["Cutaneous","Dermatophytoses (tinea), cutaneous candidiasis"],["Subcutaneous","Mycetoma, sporotrichosis, chromoblastomycosis, rhinosporidiosis"],["Systemic","Histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis"],["Opportunistic","Candidiasis, cryptococcosis, aspergillosis, mucormycosis, PCP"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Method","Examples"],["Direct microscopy","KOH mount, calcofluor white, India ink, Gram"],["Histopathology","PAS, GMS, mucicarmine"],["Culture","SDA at 25 °C and 37 °C, CHROMagar, bird seed agar"],["Identification","LPCB mount, slide culture, germ tube, MALDI-TOF"],["Antigen","CrAg, galactomannan, β-D-glucan"],["Serology/molecular","Immunodiffusion, PCR"]]]],
{i:"Fungal infections are increasing with diabetes, HIV, transplantation, cancer chemotherapy and broad-spectrum antibiotics.",f:"Specimen → KOH / special stain (rapid)\n → culture on SDA (25 °C & 37 °C) → colony + LPCB mount\n → identification (germ tube, assimilation, MALDI-TOF)\n + antigen/PCR/serology for systemic mycoses",c:"Combining direct microscopy, culture and non-culture tests allows timely diagnosis of fungal infections, which is critical for opportunistic mycoses in immunocompromised patients."});

Z("micro9",1,"m",["Dermatophytes are fungi that live on keratin (skin, hair, nails), causing ringworm (tinea) infections; they belong to three genera — Trichophyton, Microsporum and Epidermophyton.","Keratin-eating fungi that grow outward in a ring while healing in the centre — hence 'ringworm' though no worm is involved.",["Trichophyton: skin, hair, nails; Microsporum: skin, hair; Epidermophyton: skin, nails (never hair).","Sources: anthropophilic (humans), zoophilic (animals — more inflammatory), geophilic (soil).","Clinical: tinea capitis, corporis, cruris, pedis, unguium (onychomycosis), barbae; kerion; id reaction.","Lab: KOH mount (hyaline septate hyphae, arthrospores), Wood's lamp (Microsporum — green fluorescence), culture on SDA with cycloheximide; macroconidia shapes identify genus. Treatment: topical azoles, terbinafine, griseofulvin."],"Dermatophytes 'TME': Trichophyton (all), Microsporum (no nails), Epidermophyton (no hair)."],
"Dermatophytes are keratinophilic filamentous fungi causing dermatophytosis (tinea, ringworm) of skin, hair and nails; they invade only keratinised tissues and do not penetrate living tissue. Genera: Trichophyton (infects skin, hair and nails; macroconidia rare, pencil/cigar-shaped, thin smooth-walled; microconidia abundant — T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. tonsurans, T. violaceum, T. schoenleinii), Microsporum (skin and hair, not nails; macroconidia abundant, spindle-shaped, thick rough-walled; microconidia few — M. canis, M. gypseum, M. audouinii), Epidermophyton (skin and nails, not hair; macroconidia club-shaped/'beaver tail' in clusters, no microconidia — E. floccosum). Ecological classification: anthropophilic (human to human; chronic, less inflammatory — T. rubrum), zoophilic (from animals; acute, inflammatory — M. canis from cats and dogs), geophilic (soil — M. gypseum). Pathogenesis: arthroconidia adhere to keratin, germinate, and produce keratinases; inflammation at the advancing edge produces annular lesions with central clearing; hair invasion may be ectothrix (spores outside hair shaft — Microsporum) or endothrix (inside — T. tonsurans, T. violaceum) or favic (T. schoenleinii). Clinical types: tinea capitis (scalp; grey patch, black dot, kerion, favus), tinea corporis, tinea cruris, tinea pedis (athlete's foot), tinea manuum, tinea unguium (onychomycosis), tinea barbae, tinea faciei, tinea imbricata, tinea incognito (steroid-modified); dermatophytid (id) reaction. India currently faces an epidemic of chronic, recalcitrant dermatophytosis (T. mentagrophytes genotype VIII/T. indotineae) with terbinafine resistance, linked to steroid-combination creams. Laboratory diagnosis: specimens — skin scrapings from the active edge, nail clippings and subungual debris, plucked hair; direct microscopy with 10–20% KOH (40% for nails) — hyaline, branched, septate hyphae and arthroconidia; hair — ectothrix/endothrix spores; Wood's lamp — bright green fluorescence of hair infected with M. canis and M. audouinii, dull green in favus; culture on SDA with chloramphenicol and cycloheximide at 25–30 °C for up to 4 weeks; Dermatophyte Test Medium (red colour change); identification by colony, pigment, LPCB mount (macro- and microconidia), hair perforation test, urease test; PCR, MALDI-TOF. Treatment: topical azoles, terbinafine, ciclopirox; systemic terbinafine, itraconazole, griseofulvin (tinea capitis).",
[["Genera",[["Feature","Trichophyton","Microsporum","Epidermophyton"],["Infects","Skin, hair, nails","Skin, hair","Skin, nails"],["Macroconidia","Few, pencil-shaped, smooth","Many, spindle, rough, thick-walled","Club-shaped, smooth, in clusters"],["Microconidia","Abundant","Few","Absent"],["Example","T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes","M. canis","E. floccosum"]]],
["Clinical types",[["Tinea","Site"],["Capitis","Scalp, hair"],["Corporis","Body"],["Cruris","Groin"],["Pedis","Feet"],["Unguium","Nails"],["Barbae","Beard"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["KOH mount: septate hyphae, arthrospores","Wood's lamp: green fluorescence (Microsporum)","SDA with cycloheximide; DTM","LPCB: macroconidia morphology","Hair perforation, urease tests"]]],
{i:"Dermatophytoses are among the commonest skin infections in India, with rising chronic and drug-resistant forms.",f:"Arthroconidia → adhere to keratin → keratinase → hyphae spread\n → inflammation at advancing edge (ring)\nLab: scraping → KOH (hyphae) → culture SDA + cycloheximide → macroconidia → genus",c:"Dermatophytes are identified by KOH mount and culture; rational antifungal use and avoiding steroid creams are essential to control the current epidemic of chronic tinea."});

Z("micro9",2,"m",["Mycetoma (Madura foot) is a chronic infection of skin and deeper tissues, usually of the foot, caused by fungi (eumycetoma) or actinomycete bacteria (actinomycetoma), producing swelling, sinuses and grains.","Thorn-prick introduces soil organisms into the foot, which form colonies (grains) and slowly tunnel through tissue and bone, discharging granules through sinuses.",["Triad: painless tumefaction (swelling), multiple draining sinuses, grains in discharge.","Eumycetoma: fungi (Madurella mycetomatis — black grains); treated with itraconazole + surgery.","Actinomycetoma: Nocardia, Actinomadura, Streptomyces (white, yellow, red grains); treated with antibiotics (co-trimoxazole, amikacin) — better prognosis.","Lab: grains (colour, size), crushed grain KOH/Gram stain, culture on SDA and blood agar, histopathology."],"Mycetoma triad: 'Tumour, Tracts (sinuses), Tiny grains'."],
"Mycetoma is a chronic, localised, slowly progressive granulomatous infection of subcutaneous tissue, fascia and bone, typically of the foot (Madura foot — first described in Madurai), following traumatic implantation of soil organisms (thorns, splinters) in barefoot rural workers in tropical regions. It is caused by fungi (eumycetoma — Madurella mycetomatis, M. grisea, Scedosporium apiospermum/Pseudallescheria boydii, Acremonium) or aerobic actinomycetes (actinomycetoma — Nocardia brasiliensis, N. asteroides, Actinomadura madurae, A. pelletieri, Streptomyces somaliensis). Clinical triad: painless subcutaneous swelling (tumefaction), multiple sinus tracts, discharge containing grains (microcolonies of the organism); extends to bone causing osteomyelitis and deformity. Grains differ in colour: black (Madurella — eumycetoma), white/yellow (Nocardia, Actinomadura madurae, Scedosporium), red (Actinomadura pelletieri). Laboratory diagnosis: examination of discharge or deep biopsy for grains — colour, size, consistency; crushed grains in KOH show broad (2–5 µm) septate hyphae with chlamydospores (eumycetoma) or in Gram stain show thin (< 1 µm) Gram-positive branching filaments (actinomycetoma; Nocardia is modified acid-fast); culture on SDA (without cycloheximide for some) and blood agar/LJ; histopathology (Splendore–Hoeppli phenomenon around grains); PCR/sequencing; imaging for bone involvement. Treatment: actinomycetoma — prolonged antibiotics (co-trimoxazole, amikacin, dapsone, linezolid) with good response; eumycetoma — itraconazole/voriconazole plus surgical excision; amputation in advanced cases.",
[["Eumycetoma vs actinomycetoma",[["Feature","Eumycetoma","Actinomycetoma"],["Agent","True fungi","Aerobic actinomycetes (bacteria)"],["Examples","Madurella mycetomatis","Nocardia, Actinomadura, Streptomyces"],["Grain colour","Black, white","White, yellow, red"],["Filaments in grain","Broad (2–5 µm) septate hyphae","Thin (< 1 µm) branching filaments"],["Progression","Slow","Faster, more bone involvement"],["Treatment","Antifungals + surgery","Antibiotics (co-trimoxazole, amikacin)"],["Prognosis","Poorer","Better"]]]],
{c:"Mycetoma is diagnosed by examining grains; distinguishing eumycetoma from actinomycetoma is essential because treatment differs completely."});

Z("micro9",3,"m",["Histoplasma capsulatum is a dimorphic fungus found in soil contaminated with bird or bat droppings; inhaled spores cause lung infection that can spread through macrophages, especially in AIDS.","Spores from bat caves and bird roosts are inhaled; the fungus then lives inside macrophages as tiny yeasts, hiding in the lungs, liver and spleen.",["Dimorphic: mould at 25 °C (tuberculate macroconidia), yeast at 37 °C (small 2–4 µm intracellular yeasts in macrophages).","Infection by inhalation of microconidia (caves, chicken coops); not person to person.","Disease: asymptomatic, acute pulmonary, chronic cavitary (mimics TB), disseminated (AIDS — fever, hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopenia, oral ulcers, adrenal involvement).","Lab: Giemsa/GMS of marrow, blood, tissue; culture; urine/serum antigen; serology; histoplasmin skin test. Treatment: amphotericin B, itraconazole."],"Histoplasma = 'Hides in macrophages, from bird & bat droppings'."],
"Histoplasma capsulatum var. capsulatum is a thermally dimorphic fungus. In the environment (and at 25 °C) it grows as a mould with septate hyphae bearing large thick-walled tuberculate (knobby) macroconidia and small microconidia (infective); in tissue and at 37 °C it is a small (2–4 µm) oval budding yeast, found intracellularly within macrophages (resembling Leishmania amastigotes but lacking kinetoplast). Habitat: soil enriched with bird and bat guano (caves, chicken coops, old buildings); endemic in Ohio–Mississippi valleys and parts of India (West Bengal, Assam). H. capsulatum var. duboisii causes African histoplasmosis (skin and bone, larger yeasts). Infection: inhalation of microconidia; conversion to yeast in alveoli, phagocytosis by macrophages, spread to hilar nodes and RE system; cell-mediated immunity controls infection, leaving calcified granulomas. Clinical: asymptomatic (most), acute pulmonary histoplasmosis (flu-like, after heavy exposure), chronic cavitary pulmonary disease (in COPD, mimics TB), progressive disseminated histoplasmosis (in AIDS, infants, immunosuppressed — fever, weight loss, hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, pancytopenia, mucocutaneous ulcers, adrenal insufficiency), mediastinal fibrosis, ocular histoplasmosis. Laboratory diagnosis: microscopy of Giemsa/Wright-stained bone marrow, blood buffy coat, sputum, BAL, biopsy (intracellular yeasts); GMS/PAS staining of tissue; culture on SDA/BHI at 25 °C (mould with tuberculate macroconidia; slow, 2–4 weeks; BSL-3) and conversion to yeast at 37 °C; antigen detection in urine and serum (most useful in disseminated disease); serology (immunodiffusion — H and M bands, CFT); PCR; histoplasmin skin test (epidemiological). Treatment: liposomal amphotericin B for severe disease, itraconazole for mild/moderate and maintenance.",
[["Features",["Dimorphic: mould (tuberculate macroconidia) at 25 °C; yeast (2–4 µm, intracellular) at 37 °C","Source: bird/bat droppings","Inhalation; not contagious","Intracellular in macrophages"]],
["Clinical forms",["Asymptomatic (calcified granulomas)","Acute pulmonary","Chronic cavitary (mimics TB)","Disseminated (AIDS): hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopenia, ulcers, adrenal"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Giemsa/GMS: intracellular yeasts in marrow, blood, tissue","Culture: tuberculate macroconidia at 25 °C","Urine/serum antigen","Serology: immunodiffusion (H, M bands), CFT","PCR"]]],
{c:"Histoplasmosis ranges from silent infection to fatal dissemination in AIDS; demonstration of intracellular yeasts, antigen detection and culture establish the diagnosis."});

Z("micro9",4,"m",["Cryptococcus neoformans is a capsulated yeast found in pigeon droppings; inhaled, it can spread to the brain, causing meningitis, especially in AIDS.","A yeast wearing a thick slimy coat (capsule) that hides it from immune cells and shows up as a clear halo in India ink.",["Capsulated budding yeast (4–10 µm); urease +, phenol oxidase (melanin) — brown colonies on bird seed (niger seed) agar.","Source: pigeon droppings (C. neoformans), eucalyptus trees (C. gattii).","Disease: meningitis/meningoencephalitis (AIDS, CD4 < 100) — headache, raised ICP; pulmonary, skin.","Lab: CSF India ink (halo), CrAg (latex/LFA — most sensitive), culture on SDA; mucicarmine stain. Treatment: amphotericin B + flucytosine, then fluconazole."],"Crypto = 'Capsule halo in India ink, Pigeon droppings, CrAg'."],
"Cryptococcus neoformans (and C. gattii) are encapsulated basidiomycetous yeasts. Morphology: spherical budding yeast (4–10 µm, narrow-based buds) surrounded by a thick polysaccharide (glucuronoxylomannan) capsule, demonstrated by India ink or nigrosin negative staining (clear halo) and mucicarmine (red) in tissue. It does not form pseudohyphae. Culture: SDA at 25 °C and 37 °C (inhibited by cycloheximide) forms mucoid cream colonies; on bird seed (niger seed, Staib) agar forms brown colonies due to phenol oxidase producing melanin; urease positive; assimilates inositol; does not ferment sugars. Habitat: C. neoformans — soil enriched with pigeon droppings worldwide; C. gattii — eucalyptus and other trees in tropics. Virulence factors: capsule (antiphagocytic, immunosuppressive), melanin (antioxidant), urease, growth at 37 °C. Infection by inhalation of desiccated yeasts or basidiospores; primary pulmonary infection usually subclinical; haematogenous spread to CNS (predilection due to absence of complement and alternative-pathway factors in CSF and dopamine for melanin synthesis). Disease: cryptococcal meningitis/meningoencephalitis — subacute headache, fever, altered mentation, raised intracranial pressure, cranial nerve palsies; most common in AIDS (CD4 < 100), also transplant, steroids, lymphoma; C. gattii affects immunocompetent hosts; pulmonary nodules, skin lesions (molluscum-like), bone, prostate (reservoir). Laboratory diagnosis: CSF — raised opening pressure, lymphocytes, raised protein, low glucose; India ink preparation (capsulated budding yeasts; sensitivity 70–80%); cryptococcal antigen by latex agglutination or lateral flow assay (> 95% sensitivity; also serum — screening in HIV); culture on SDA, bird seed agar; blood culture; histopathology with mucicarmine, GMS; MALDI-TOF; PCR. Treatment: induction with amphotericin B (liposomal) plus flucytosine, consolidation and maintenance with fluconazole; therapeutic lumbar punctures for raised ICP; serum CrAg screening and pre-emptive fluconazole in advanced HIV.",
[["Identification",["Capsulated budding yeast; India ink halo","Mucicarmine-positive capsule","Urease +, phenol oxidase + (brown on bird seed agar)","Inhibited by cycloheximide","No pseudohyphae"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Test","Comment"],["India ink (CSF)","Rapid; 70–80% sensitive"],["CrAg (latex, LFA)","> 95% sensitive; CSF and serum"],["Culture","SDA, bird seed agar"],["Histology","Mucicarmine, GMS"]]]],
{c:"Cryptococcal meningitis is a leading cause of death in advanced HIV; CrAg testing and India ink diagnose it, and combination antifungal therapy with ICP control saves lives."});

Z("micro9",5,"m",["Candida is a yeast-like fungus that normally lives in the mouth, gut and vagina; when defences fall it causes thrush, vaginitis, skin and nail infections, and serious bloodstream infections.","Candida is a normally peaceful resident; antibiotics, diabetes, steroids or HIV remove its competitors and guards, letting it overgrow.",["Budding yeast with pseudohyphae; Gram-positive; germ tube test positive for C. albicans (within 2 h in serum); chlamydospores on cornmeal agar.","Predisposing: antibiotics, diabetes, pregnancy, OCP, steroids, HIV, catheters, neutropenia, ICU.","Diseases: oral thrush, oesophagitis (AIDS), vulvovaginitis (curdy discharge), intertrigo, paronychia, onychomycosis, candidaemia, endocarditis, UTI.","Lab: Gram/KOH (yeast + pseudohyphae), culture on SDA, CHROMagar species ID, germ tube, β-D-glucan; C. auris — multidrug resistant, hospital outbreaks."],"C. albicans = 'Germ tube + in 2 hours, Chlamydospores'."],
"Candida species are yeast-like fungi: oval budding yeasts (3–5 µm) that also form pseudohyphae and, in C. albicans, true hyphae; Gram-positive. They are commensals of the oral cavity, GI tract, vagina and skin. Species: C. albicans (commonest), C. tropicalis, C. parapsilosis (catheters, neonates), C. glabrata (fluconazole-resistant), C. krusei (intrinsically fluconazole resistant), Candida auris (multidrug-resistant, persistent in hospital environment, causes outbreaks). Virulence: dimorphism (hyphae invade), adhesins (Als proteins), proteases, phospholipases, biofilm formation on devices, phenotypic switching. Predisposing factors: extremes of age, broad-spectrum antibiotics, diabetes mellitus, pregnancy, oral contraceptives, corticosteroids, HIV and other immunodeficiencies, neutropenia, malignancy, indwelling catheters, parenteral nutrition, surgery, ICU stay, dentures, moisture. Clinical: mucocutaneous — oral thrush (white curd-like removable patches), angular cheilitis, oesophagitis (AIDS-defining), vulvovaginitis (itching, thick curdy white discharge), balanitis, intertrigo, napkin dermatitis, paronychia and onychomycosis, chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis; systemic — candidaemia, disseminated candidiasis, endocarditis (IV drug users, prosthetic valves), endophthalmitis, UTI, peritonitis, hepatosplenic candidiasis. Laboratory diagnosis: direct microscopy (Gram or KOH) — budding yeasts with pseudohyphae (indicating invasion); culture on SDA (creamy pasty colonies), blood culture; identification — germ tube test (C. albicans forms germ tubes in serum at 37 °C within 2 hours — Reynolds–Braude phenomenon), chlamydospores on cornmeal agar with Tween 80 (Dalmau plate), CHROMagar Candida (colour differentiation), sugar assimilation and fermentation, MALDI-TOF (needed for C. auris); antigen/β-D-glucan, mannan; PCR; antifungal susceptibility testing. Treatment: topical azoles/nystatin; fluconazole; echinocandins for candidaemia; amphotericin B.",
[["Identification",["Gram-positive budding yeast with pseudohyphae","SDA: creamy colonies","Germ tube test (C. albicans)","Chlamydospores on cornmeal agar","CHROMagar: species colours","MALDI-TOF (C. auris)"]],
["Clinical spectrum",[["Type","Examples"],["Mucosal","Oral thrush, oesophagitis, vulvovaginitis"],["Cutaneous","Intertrigo, paronychia, onychomycosis"],["Systemic","Candidaemia, endocarditis, endophthalmitis, UTI"]]]],
{c:"Candida is an opportunist whose infections reflect host predisposition; species identification and susceptibility testing are increasingly important with resistant species like C. auris."});

Z("micro9",6,"m",["Aspergillus is a ubiquitous mould whose airborne spores can cause allergy (ABPA), fungus balls in old lung cavities (aspergilloma), or deadly invasive infection in neutropenic patients.","Aspergillus spores float everywhere; healthy lungs clear them, allergic lungs overreact, old cavities let them grow into balls, and weak immunity lets them invade vessels.",["Septate hyphae (3–6 µm) with acute-angle (45°) dichotomous branching; conidiophore with vesicle and phialides ('aspergillum' — holy water sprinkler).","Species: A. fumigatus (commonest), A. flavus (aflatoxin; sinusitis in India), A. niger (otomycosis — black).","Spectrum: allergic (ABPA, asthma, allergic sinusitis), colonising (aspergilloma — air crescent sign), invasive (neutropenia — angioinvasion, halo sign), chronic pulmonary aspergillosis.","Lab: KOH/GMS of sputum/tissue, culture on SDA (no cycloheximide), galactomannan, β-D-glucan, PCR, IgE and precipitins (ABPA). Treatment: voriconazole; itraconazole + steroids for ABPA."],"Aspergillus = 'Acute angle, septate, A. fumigatus, galactomannan'."],
"Aspergillus species are saprophytic, ubiquitous filamentous fungi in soil, decaying vegetation, air and hospital construction dust; infection is by inhalation of conidia. Morphology: hyaline, narrow (3–6 µm), septate hyphae with regular dichotomous branching at acute (45°) angles; in culture, conidiophores end in a swollen vesicle bearing one or two rows of phialides producing chains of conidia. Species identification by colony colour and microscopy: A. fumigatus (blue-green, smoky; commonest pathogen; thermotolerant), A. flavus (yellow-green; aflatoxin producer; commonest in sinusitis and keratitis in India), A. niger (black; otomycosis), A. terreus (cinnamon; amphotericin-resistant). Diseases: allergic — allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (in asthma and cystic fibrosis; wheeze, fleeting infiltrates, central bronchiectasis, eosinophilia, raised total and specific IgE, precipitins), allergic fungal sinusitis, extrinsic allergic alveolitis; colonisation — aspergilloma (fungus ball in pre-existing cavities such as healed TB; haemoptysis; air-crescent sign), otomycosis; chronic pulmonary aspergillosis; invasive aspergillosis — in neutropenia, haematological malignancy, transplant, steroids, COVID-19 ICU patients: angioinvasive pneumonia with haemorrhagic infarcts (halo sign on CT), dissemination to brain, invasive sinusitis, keratitis (after trauma with vegetable matter), endocarditis; mycotoxicosis (aflatoxin and hepatocellular carcinoma). Laboratory diagnosis: direct microscopy (KOH, calcofluor) of sputum, BAL, tissue; histopathology (GMS, PAS — septate acute-angle branching hyphae invading vessels); culture on SDA without cycloheximide (rapid growth in 2–3 days) with LPCB mount; galactomannan antigen in serum/BAL (ELISA), β-D-glucan; PCR; serology — IgG precipitins, specific and total IgE (ABPA), skin prick test; imaging. Treatment: voriconazole (invasive), isavuconazole, liposomal amphotericin B, echinocandins; surgery for aspergilloma; itraconazole plus steroids for ABPA.",
[["Clinical spectrum",[["Category","Disease","Host"],["Allergic","ABPA, allergic sinusitis","Asthma, CF"],["Saprophytic","Aspergilloma, otomycosis","Old cavities, ear"],["Chronic","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis","Lung disease"],["Invasive","Invasive pulmonary, sinusitis, CNS","Neutropenia, transplant"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["KOH/GMS: septate acute-angle hyphae","Culture: SDA; vesicle, phialides","Galactomannan, β-D-glucan","PCR; IgE, precipitins for ABPA"]]],
{c:"Aspergillus causes a spectrum from allergy to fatal invasion; host immunity determines the form, and antigen tests with imaging enable early treatment of invasive disease."});

Z("micro9",7,"m",["Pneumocystis jirovecii is a fungus that causes severe pneumonia (PCP) in people with very weak immunity, especially AIDS with CD4 < 200.","An atypical fungus that quietly fills the air sacs with foamy material, starving the blood of oxygen.",["Atypical fungus (lacks ergosterol — amphotericin ineffective); cannot be cultured.","Forms: trophic forms and cysts (asci with 8 spores).","PCP: dry cough, fever, progressive dyspnoea, hypoxia; bilateral interstitial infiltrates ('ground glass'); high LDH.","Lab: induced sputum/BAL — GMS (cysts, crushed ping-pong ball), Giemsa (trophic forms), DFA (best), PCR, β-D-glucan. Treatment/prophylaxis: co-trimoxazole."],"PCP = 'Pneumonia, CD4 < 200, co-trimoxazole Prevents'."],
"Pneumocystis jirovecii (formerly P. carinii) is an atypical fungus (based on rRNA, mitochondrial DNA and cell wall β-glucan) that lacks ergosterol (so not susceptible to amphotericin and azoles) and cannot be cultured in vitro. Life cycle stages: trophic forms (1–4 µm, pleomorphic, attach to type I pneumocytes) and cysts (asci, 5–8 µm, containing eight intracystic bodies). Transmission is airborne; most children are exposed early; disease arises from new infection or reactivation in immunocompromised hosts. Pneumocystis pneumonia occurs in AIDS (CD4 < 200; AIDS-defining), haematological malignancies, transplant recipients, corticosteroid therapy, malnourished infants. Pathology: alveoli filled with foamy, frothy eosinophilic exudate (honeycomb) containing organisms, interstitial plasma cell infiltration, hypoxaemia. Clinical: subacute onset of fever, dry cough, progressive dyspnoea, tachypnoea, hypoxia out of proportion to signs; chest X-ray — bilateral perihilar interstitial infiltrates; HRCT — ground-glass opacities; pneumothorax; raised LDH. Laboratory diagnosis: specimens — induced sputum, bronchoalveolar lavage, lung biopsy; staining — Grocott methenamine silver (cyst walls; 'crushed ping-pong balls'), toluidine blue O, calcofluor white (cysts); Giemsa/Wright (trophic forms and intracystic bodies); direct immunofluorescence with monoclonal antibodies (most sensitive staining); PCR (very sensitive; quantitative to distinguish colonisation); serum β-D-glucan (high negative predictive value). Treatment: co-trimoxazole (high dose) with corticosteroids if PaO2 < 70 mmHg; alternatives — pentamidine, primaquine + clindamycin, atovaquone. Prophylaxis: co-trimoxazole when CD4 < 200.",
[["Diagnosis",["Specimen: induced sputum, BAL","GMS: cysts (crushed ping-pong ball)","Giemsa: trophic forms","DFA: most sensitive stain","PCR, β-D-glucan","Cannot be cultured"]]],
{c:"PCP is a preventable AIDS-defining pneumonia; staining of BAL or induced sputum and PCR confirm it, and co-trimoxazole is used for both treatment and prophylaxis."});

Z("micro9",8,"m",["Mucormycosis is an aggressive infection by moulds of the order Mucorales (Rhizopus, Mucor), mainly in uncontrolled diabetes, that invades blood vessels and causes black dead tissue in the nose, eyes and brain.","The fungus grows along blood vessels like roots through pipes, blocking blood supply and turning tissue black.",["Broad (6–25 µm), ribbon-like, aseptate/pauciseptate hyphae with right-angle branching.","Risk: DKA/uncontrolled diabetes, steroids (COVID-19), neutropenia, iron overload (deferoxamine), transplant.","Forms: rhino-orbito-cerebral (commonest), pulmonary, cutaneous, GI, disseminated; black eschar on palate/nose.","Lab: KOH/calcofluor of tissue (rapid), histopathology (angioinvasion), culture on SDA (fast cottony growth, sporangia); treat with liposomal amphotericin B + surgical debridement + glucose control."],"Mucor = 'Mega ribbons, Missing septa, Melted (black) tissue in diabetics'."],
"Mucormycosis (zygomycosis) is an acute, angioinvasive infection caused by fungi of the order Mucorales — Rhizopus (commonest, especially R. arrhizus), Mucor, Lichtheimia, Rhizomucor, Apophysomyces (cutaneous, India), Cunninghamella. They are ubiquitous in soil and decaying matter. Hyphae are broad (6–25 µm), thin-walled, ribbon-like, aseptate or pauciseptate, with irregular wide-angle (90°) branching; in culture they grow rapidly (cotton-candy colonies filling the plate) with sporangiophores, sporangia containing sporangiospores, and rhizoids (Rhizopus). Risk factors: diabetes mellitus especially ketoacidosis (acidosis releases free iron; fungal ketone reductase), corticosteroids (large outbreak in India during COVID-19 — 'black fungus'), neutropenia, haematological malignancies, transplant, iron overload and deferoxamine therapy, trauma and burns. Infection by inhalation of spores, or inoculation. Pathogenesis: hyphae invade blood vessels, causing thrombosis, infarction and tissue necrosis, with rapid spread. Clinical forms: rhino-orbito-cerebral (sinusitis, facial pain and swelling, black necrotic eschar on nasal turbinate or palate, orbital cellulitis, proptosis, ophthalmoplegia, vision loss, cavernous sinus thrombosis, brain abscess), pulmonary (neutropenia), cutaneous (trauma, burns), gastrointestinal (malnourished infants), disseminated. Laboratory diagnosis: urgent direct microscopy of tissue or aspirate with KOH/calcofluor white (broad aseptate ribbon-like hyphae); histopathology (H&E, PAS, GMS — angioinvasion, necrosis); culture on SDA without cycloheximide (rapid growth; tissue should be minced, not homogenised); PCR, MALDI-TOF; imaging (CT/MRI). β-D-glucan and galactomannan are negative. Treatment: urgent surgical debridement, high-dose liposomal amphotericin B, then posaconazole or isavuconazole; correction of hyperglycaemia and acidosis, reduction of immunosuppression.",
[["Mucor vs Aspergillus in tissue",[["Feature","Mucorales","Aspergillus"],["Hyphal width","Broad (6–25 µm)","Narrow (3–6 µm)"],["Septa","Absent/sparse","Regular"],["Branching","Irregular, 90°","Dichotomous, 45°"],["Galactomannan","Negative","Positive"],["Drug","Amphotericin B, posaconazole","Voriconazole"]]]],
{c:"Mucormycosis is a rapidly fatal angioinvasive mycosis; urgent KOH microscopy, surgery, amphotericin B and diabetes control are critical."});

Z("micro9",9,"m",["Sporotrichosis is a subcutaneous fungal infection ('rose gardener's disease') by dimorphic Sporothrix schenckii that spreads along lymphatics; rhinosporidiosis is a chronic polypoid infection of the nose and eye caused by Rhinosporidium seeberi, common in India.","Sporothrix enters through a thorn prick and climbs the lymphatic ladder, forming a chain of nodules; Rhinosporidium grows strawberry-like polyps in the nose.",["Sporothrix: dimorphic; thorn/splinter injury; lymphocutaneous nodules along lymphatics; cigar-shaped yeasts and asteroid bodies in tissue; treat with itraconazole/potassium iodide.","Rhinosporidium seeberi: aquatic protist (Mesomycetozoea), not a true fungus; cannot be cultured.","Rhinosporidiosis: friable, vascular, strawberry-like nasal/conjunctival polyps that bleed; bathing in ponds (South India, Sri Lanka).","Rhinosporidium diagnosis: histology — large sporangia (up to 300 µm) with endospores; treatment surgical excision + cautery, dapsone."],"Sporotrichosis = 'Rose thorn → lymphatic chain'; Rhinosporidiosis = 'Strawberry nose polyp'."],
"Sporotrichosis is a subcutaneous mycosis caused by the thermally dimorphic fungus Sporothrix schenckii complex (including S. brasiliensis, transmitted by cats). It lives on plants, soil, moss, thorns and wood; infection follows traumatic inoculation (rose thorns, splinters — 'rose gardener's disease'). Clinical forms: lymphocutaneous (commonest; painless nodule or ulcer at the inoculation site on hand/arm, followed by a chain of nodules along draining lymphatics — sporotrichoid spread), fixed cutaneous, and rarely pulmonary, osteoarticular or disseminated (immunocompromised, alcoholics). Laboratory diagnosis: tissue shows few cigar-shaped yeasts and asteroid bodies (yeast surrounded by eosinophilic Splendore–Hoeppli material); culture on SDA at 25 °C gives a mould with delicate hyphae and conidia arranged in 'flower-like' (rosette) clusters, converting to yeast at 37 °C on BHI; serology, PCR. Treatment: itraconazole; saturated solution of potassium iodide; amphotericin B for disseminated. Rhinosporidiosis is a chronic granulomatous infection caused by Rhinosporidium seeberi, now classified as an aquatic protist (Mesomycetozoea, Ichthyosporea) rather than a fungus; it cannot be cultured. It is endemic in South India (Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Odisha) and Sri Lanka, associated with bathing in stagnant ponds. Lesions are soft, pink to red, friable, highly vascular, pedunculated or sessile polyps (strawberry-like with white dots — sporangia) in the nose and nasopharynx (commonest), conjunctiva, lacrimal sac, and rarely larynx, genitalia and skin; they bleed easily and cause nasal obstruction and epistaxis. Diagnosis by histopathology: large thick-walled sporangia (up to 300 µm) packed with thousands of endospores (5–10 µm) and trophocytes in fibrovascular stroma (stain with PAS, GMS, mucicarmine). Treatment: wide surgical excision with electrocautery of the base; dapsone to reduce recurrence.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Sporotrichosis","Rhinosporidiosis"],["Agent","Sporothrix schenckii (dimorphic fungus)","Rhinosporidium seeberi (protist)"],["Source","Thorns, plants, soil, cats","Stagnant water"],["Lesions","Lymphocutaneous nodules","Polyps of nose, conjunctiva"],["Tissue form","Cigar-shaped yeasts, asteroid bodies","Sporangia with endospores"],["Culture","Yes (rosette conidia)","No"],["Treatment","Itraconazole, KI","Surgical excision, dapsone"]]]],
{c:"Both are chronic subcutaneous infections with distinctive presentations; Sporothrix is cultured and treated with antifungals, while Rhinosporidium needs histology and surgery."});

Z("micro10",0,"m",["Urinary tract infection is infection anywhere along the urinary tract — bladder (cystitis) or kidney (pyelonephritis) — most often by E. coli entering from the urethra.","Gut bacteria climb up the short female urethra like ants up a straw; anything that blocks flow (stones, catheter, enlarged prostate) gives them a foothold.",["Commonest cause: E. coli (UPEC with P fimbriae); others Klebsiella, Proteus, Enterococcus, S. saprophyticus (young women), Pseudomonas (catheter).","Routes: ascending (commonest), haematogenous, lymphatic.","Specimen: clean-catch midstream urine, catheter urine, suprapubic aspiration; process within 1 h or refrigerate.","Significant bacteriuria (Kass): ≥ 10⁵ CFU/mL; lower counts significant with symptoms, catheter or suprapubic sample. Pyuria > 10 WBC/mm³."],"UTI organisms 'KEEPS': Klebsiella, E. coli, Enterococcus, Proteus/Pseudomonas, S. saprophyticus."],
"Urinary tract infection is microbial invasion of any part of the urinary tract — urethritis, cystitis (lower UTI), pyelonephritis (upper UTI), prostatitis — classified as uncomplicated or complicated (structural/functional abnormalities, catheters, pregnancy, men, diabetes). Women are more affected because of the short urethra. Routes: ascending (most common, from perineal flora), haematogenous (S. aureus, Candida, Salmonella, M. tuberculosis), lymphatic. Agents: E. coli (70–80% of community UTI; uropathogenic E. coli with P fimbriae, type 1 fimbriae, haemolysin, aerobactin, K antigen), Klebsiella, Proteus (urease, stones), Enterobacter, Citrobacter, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Enterococcus faecalis, Staphylococcus saprophyticus (young sexually active women), S. aureus, Candida (catheterised, diabetic). Predisposing factors: female sex, sexual activity, pregnancy, obstruction (stones, BPH, strictures), vesicoureteric reflux, catheterisation and instrumentation, diabetes, neurogenic bladder, immunosuppression. Clinical: cystitis — dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain, haematuria; pyelonephritis — fever, chills, flank pain, vomiting; asymptomatic bacteriuria (treat in pregnancy). Laboratory diagnosis: specimen — clean-catch midstream urine (first morning), catheter urine (from port, never bag), suprapubic aspirate (infants); transport within 1 hour or refrigerate at 4 °C (or boric acid); screening — microscopy for pus cells (> 10/mm³ uncentrifuged), bacteria, casts; Gram stain (1 bacterium/oil field ≈ 10⁵/mL); dipstick nitrite (Griess) and leucocyte esterase; culture — semi-quantitative with calibrated loop (0.001 or 0.01 mL) on CLED or blood + MacConkey agar; Kass criteria — ≥ 10⁵ CFU/mL of a single organism = significant bacteriuria; 10³–10⁵ significant in symptomatic women, men, catheter samples; any growth in suprapubic aspirate significant; mixed growth suggests contamination; identification and antimicrobial susceptibility testing (ESBL detection). Imaging for recurrent/complicated UTI. Treatment: nitrofurantoin, fosfomycin, co-trimoxazole for cystitis; ceftriaxone or aminoglycosides for pyelonephritis; carbapenems for ESBL producers.",
[["Agents",[["Setting","Organisms"],["Community","E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus, S. saprophyticus"],["Hospital/catheter","E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Enterococcus, Candida"],["Haematogenous","S. aureus, Salmonella, M. tuberculosis"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Clean-catch midstream urine; process within 1 h","Microscopy: pus cells > 10/mm³, bacteria","Dipstick: nitrite, leucocyte esterase","Semi-quantitative culture (CLED/MacConkey)","Significant bacteriuria ≥ 10⁵ CFU/mL (Kass)","Identification and AST"]]],
{c:"Proper collection and quantitative culture are the basis of UTI diagnosis; local resistance data guide empirical therapy."});

Z("micro10",1,"m",["Meningitis is inflammation of the brain's coverings, caused by bacteria, viruses, TB or fungi; the causative organisms depend on age and immune status, and CSF examination identifies the cause.","Different age groups attract different 'gangs' of germs — newborns get gut and birth-canal bacteria, adults get pneumococcus and meningococcus.",["Neonates: group B strep, E. coli K1, Listeria; children: pneumococcus, meningococcus, Hib (pre-vaccine); adults: pneumococcus, meningococcus; elderly: pneumococcus, Listeria.","Chronic: M. tuberculosis, Cryptococcus; viral: enteroviruses, HSV, mumps.","CSF bacterial: turbid, neutrophils, ↑ protein, ↓ glucose; viral: lymphocytes, normal glucose; TB: lymphocytes, ↑↑ protein, ↓ glucose.","Lab: Gram stain, culture, antigen detection (latex), India ink/CrAg, CBNAAT, multiplex PCR; blood culture."],"Meningitis by age: 'GEL' neonates (GBS, E. coli, Listeria); 'PM' adults (Pneumo, Meningo)."],
"Meningitis is inflammation of the leptomeninges (pia and arachnoid) and CSF. Acute bacterial (pyogenic) meningitis agents by age: neonates (< 1 month) — Streptococcus agalactiae (GBS), E. coli K1, Listeria monocytogenes, Klebsiella; infants and children — S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae type b (reduced by vaccination); adults — S. pneumoniae (commonest), N. meningitidis; elderly and immunocompromised — S. pneumoniae, Listeria, Gram-negative bacilli; post-neurosurgical/shunt — S. aureus, CoNS, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter. Aseptic (viral) meningitis — enteroviruses (commonest), HSV-2, mumps, VZV, HIV, arboviruses. Chronic meningitis — Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Cryptococcus neoformans, Treponema pallidum, Brucella, Leptospira; parasitic — Angiostrongylus (eosinophilic), Naegleria (primary amoebic meningoencephalitis). Pathogenesis: colonisation of nasopharynx → bloodstream invasion (capsule) → crossing blood–brain barrier → inflammation (cytokines), cerebral oedema, raised ICP, vasculitis. Clinical: fever, headache, neck stiffness, photophobia, vomiting, altered sensorium, seizures; Kernig and Brudzinski signs; petechial rash in meningococcaemia; infants — irritability, bulging fontanelle, poor feeding. Laboratory diagnosis: lumbar puncture (after excluding raised ICP); CSF — opening pressure, appearance, cell count and differential, protein, glucose (CSF:blood ratio); Gram stain (Gram-positive diplococci — pneumococcus; Gram-negative diplococci — meningococcus; Gram-negative coccobacilli — Hib; Gram-positive bacilli — Listeria); culture on blood and chocolate agar; antigen detection by latex agglutination; ZN/auramine stain, CBNAAT, culture for TB; ADA; India ink and CrAg for Cryptococcus; viral PCR (enterovirus, HSV); multiplex PCR panels; blood culture; CT/MRI for complications. Empirical treatment: ceftriaxone ± vancomycin, ampicillin for Listeria; dexamethasone; chemoprophylaxis for meningococcal and Hib contacts.",
[["Agents by age",[["Age","Organisms"],["Neonates","GBS, E. coli K1, Listeria"],["1 month–5 years","Pneumococcus, meningococcus, Hib"],["Adults","Pneumococcus, meningococcus"],["Elderly/immunocompromised","Pneumococcus, Listeria, Gram-negatives, Cryptococcus"],["Post-surgery","S. aureus, CoNS, Gram-negatives"]]],
["CSF findings",[["Type","Cells","Protein","Glucose"],["Bacterial","Neutrophils ↑↑","↑↑","↓↓"],["Viral","Lymphocytes","Mild ↑","Normal"],["Tuberculous","Lymphocytes","↑↑↑","↓"],["Fungal","Lymphocytes","↑","↓"]]]],
{c:"The cause of meningitis is predicted by age and confirmed by CSF microscopy, culture, antigen and molecular tests; prompt empirical therapy is life-saving."});

Z("micro10",2,"m",["Sexually transmitted diseases are infections spread through sexual contact, caused by bacteria (gonorrhoea, syphilis, chlamydia, chancroid, donovanosis), viruses (HIV, HSV, HPV, HBV), protozoa (Trichomonas) and others.","STIs are grouped by what they cause: discharge (urethral/vaginal), ulcers (genital sores), warts, or no symptoms — syndromic management treats these groups.",["Urethral discharge: N. gonorrhoeae, C. trachomatis, Mycoplasma genitalium, Trichomonas.","Genital ulcer: syphilis (painless, indurated), chancroid (H. ducreyi — painful, soft), herpes (painful vesicles), LGV, donovanosis (Klebsiella granulomatis — beefy red, Donovan bodies).","Vaginal discharge: Trichomonas, Candida, bacterial vaginosis (clue cells, whiff test).","Lab: Gram stain, NAAT, dark-ground, VDRL/TPHA, Tzanck/PCR, wet mount, HIV and HBV serology; syndromic case management (NACO colour-coded kits)."],"Painful ulcers: Herpes & Chancroid; painless: Syphilis, LGV, Donovanosis."],
"Sexually transmitted infections are caused by more than 30 pathogens. Bacterial: Neisseria gonorrhoeae (gonorrhoea), Chlamydia trachomatis D–K (urethritis, cervicitis, PID) and L1–L3 (LGV), Treponema pallidum (syphilis), Haemophilus ducreyi (chancroid — painful, soft, ragged ulcers with suppurative bubo; school of fish on Gram stain), Klebsiella granulomatis (donovanosis/granuloma inguinale — painless, beefy-red, bleeding ulcers; Donovan bodies — safety-pin bipolar bodies in macrophages), Mycoplasma genitalium, Ureaplasma; Gardnerella and anaerobes in bacterial vaginosis. Viral: HIV, HSV-2/HSV-1 (genital herpes), HPV (genital warts — types 6, 11; cancers — 16, 18), HBV, HCV, molluscum contagiosum, mpox. Protozoal: Trichomonas vaginalis. Fungal: Candida (vulvovaginitis — not strictly STI). Ectoparasites: Phthirus pubis, Sarcoptes scabiei. Syndromes: urethral discharge, vaginal discharge, genital ulcer disease, lower abdominal pain (PID), inguinal bubo, scrotal swelling, neonatal conjunctivitis. Laboratory diagnosis: urethral/cervical discharge — Gram stain (intracellular Gram-negative diplococci), culture on Thayer–Martin, NAAT for gonococcus and chlamydia; vaginal discharge — wet mount (Trichomonas, clue cells), KOH (Candida, whiff test), pH, Gram stain (Nugent score for BV); ulcers — dark-ground microscopy (T. pallidum), Tzanck smear and PCR (HSV), Gram stain and culture on enriched media (H. ducreyi), tissue smear with Giemsa (Donovan bodies), NAAT (LGV); serology — VDRL/RPR and TPHA (syphilis), HIV, HBsAg, HCV. Syndromic case management (WHO/NACO) uses colour-coded kits when laboratory facilities are limited. Prevention: condoms, partner notification and treatment, HPV and HBV vaccination, screening in antenatal care.",
[["Genital ulcers",[["Disease","Agent","Ulcer","Lymph nodes"],["Syphilis","T. pallidum","Painless, indurated, clean","Painless, rubbery"],["Chancroid","H. ducreyi","Painful, soft, ragged","Painful, suppurative bubo"],["Genital herpes","HSV-2","Painful multiple vesicles/erosions","Tender"],["LGV","C. trachomatis L1–3","Transient painless","Painful buboes, groove sign"],["Donovanosis","K. granulomatis","Painless, beefy red, bleeds","Pseudobubo"]]],
["Discharge syndromes",[["Syndrome","Agents"],["Urethral discharge","Gonococcus, Chlamydia, M. genitalium, Trichomonas"],["Vaginal discharge","Trichomonas, Candida, bacterial vaginosis, gonococcus, chlamydia"]]]],
{c:"STIs are diagnosed by targeted microscopy, culture, NAAT and serology, with syndromic management where labs are unavailable; partner treatment and prevention are integral."});

Z("micro10",3,"m",["The Stokes disc diffusion method tests antibiotic sensitivity by growing the patient's organism and a known sensitive control strain on the same plate and comparing the zones around antibiotic discs.","Like a race between the patient's bacterium and a standard 'reference' bacterium: if the patient's organism resists the antibiotic much more than the control, it is called resistant.",["Plate: test organism on one part, control strain (e.g., S. aureus ATCC 25923, E. coli ATCC 25922) on the other; discs placed between them.","Zones compared: sensitive (test zone ≥ control or within 3 mm smaller), intermediate (> 3 mm smaller but > 3 mm wide), resistant (≤ 2–3 mm zone).","Advantage: built-in control for each plate; disadvantage: less standardised than Kirby–Bauer (CLSI/EUCAST) which uses fixed zone diameters.","Kirby–Bauer: Mueller–Hinton agar, 0.5 McFarland inoculum, standard discs, zone diameter interpretive charts."],"Stokes = 'Side-by-side with Standard strain'."],
"The Stokes method is a comparative disc diffusion antibiotic susceptibility test in which the test organism and a standard control strain of known susceptibility are inoculated on the same plate. Procedure: a nutrient-rich agar plate (Mueller–Hinton or Isosensitest) is inoculated with the control organism (S. aureus NCTC 6571/ATCC 25923, E. coli NCTC 10418/ATCC 25922, P. aeruginosa ATCC 27853) in the outer or central band and the test organism in the remaining part, leaving a 2–3 mm gap where antibiotic discs are placed at the junction (or the test in the middle and controls on either side). After overnight incubation at 37 °C, the radius of the zone of inhibition (from disc edge to zone edge) is measured for both organisms. Interpretation: sensitive — test zone radius equal to, wider than, or not more than 3 mm smaller than the control; intermediate (moderately sensitive) — zone radius more than 3 mm but smaller than the control by more than 3 mm; resistant — zone radius of 2 mm or less. Advantages: internal control on each plate compensates for variations in medium, inoculum and disc potency; useful in laboratories without standardised conditions. Disadvantages: less reproducible and standardised than the Kirby–Bauer method, more subjective. The Kirby–Bauer (CLSI) method uses a standardised inoculum (0.5 McFarland), Mueller–Hinton agar of 4 mm depth, standard disc concentrations and interpretive zone-diameter breakpoints; other AST methods include broth/agar dilution (MIC — gold standard), E-test (gradient strips) and automated systems (VITEK 2).",
[["Interpretation (zone radius)",[["Category","Criterion"],["Sensitive","Test ≥ control, or ≤ 3 mm smaller"],["Intermediate","> 3 mm smaller than control, but > 2 mm"],["Resistant","≤ 2 mm"]]],
["Stokes vs Kirby–Bauer",[["Feature","Stokes","Kirby–Bauer"],["Control","On same plate","Separate QC strains"],["Interpretation","Comparison with control","Standard zone diameter charts"],["Standardisation","Less","High (CLSI/EUCAST)"]]]],
{c:"The Stokes method provides an internally controlled comparison of susceptibility; standardised Kirby–Bauer and MIC methods are now preferred for reporting."});

Z("micro10",4,"m",["Sepsis is a life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by the body's dysregulated response to infection; blood culture is the key test to identify the organism in the bloodstream.","Bacteria in the blood are like intruders in the city's main roads — catching them early (blood culture) before they spread chaos (shock) is crucial.",["Collect 2–3 sets from separate sites before antibiotics, with strict asepsis (chlorhexidine-alcohol).","Volume: adults 8–10 mL per bottle (aerobic + anaerobic); children 1–3 mL; blood:broth 1:5 to 1:10.","Automated systems (BacT/ALERT, BACTEC) detect CO2; incubate up to 5 days; Gram stain of positive bottle guides therapy; MALDI-TOF, rapid PCR.","Common organisms: S. aureus, E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Salmonella Typhi, Candida; contaminants: CoNS, diphtheroids, Bacillus (single bottle)."],"Blood culture '2-3 sets, 10 mL, before antibiotics'."],
"Sepsis (Sepsis-3) is life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection (increase in SOFA score ≥ 2); septic shock is sepsis requiring vasopressors with lactate > 2 mmol/L despite fluids. Bacteraemia is presence of viable bacteria in blood (transient, intermittent, continuous — endocarditis). Common agents: community — E. coli, S. aureus, S. pneumoniae, Salmonella Typhi, N. meningitidis, Klebsiella; hospital — Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, MRSA, CoNS (lines), Enterococcus, Candida; neonatal — Klebsiella, E. coli, GBS. Pathogenesis: PAMPs (endotoxin, peptidoglycan) activate TLRs → cytokine storm (TNF, IL-1, IL-6), complement, coagulation (DIC), endothelial dysfunction, capillary leak, vasodilatation, myocardial depression → multi-organ failure; immunosuppression follows. Blood culture: indications — fever with suspected bacteraemia, sepsis, endocarditis, PUO, neonatal sepsis; collect before antibiotics, at onset of fever or chills (any time for continuous bacteraemia); skin disinfection with 2% chlorhexidine in alcohol or povidone-iodine (dry time), disinfect bottle tops; two or three sets from separate venepuncture sites (each set = aerobic + anaerobic bottle); volume 8–10 mL per bottle in adults (most important determinant of yield), 1–3 mL in children (paediatric bottles); avoid drawing from existing lines except paired with peripheral for CLABSI (differential time to positivity > 2 h). Media: brain–heart infusion or trypticase soy broth with sodium polyanethol sulphonate (anticoagulant, inactivates aminoglycosides and complement), resins to neutralise antibiotics; Castaneda biphasic for Brucella. Processing: automated continuous monitoring systems (BacT/ALERT, BACTEC) incubate for 5 days and detect CO2 production; positive bottles are Gram-stained (immediate report to clinician), subcultured on blood, chocolate and MacConkey agar; rapid identification by MALDI-TOF and multiplex PCR panels; antimicrobial susceptibility testing; manual method — subcultures at 24 h, 48 h and 7 days. Interpretation: growth of the same organism in multiple sets or known pathogens is significant; CoNS, diphtheroids, Bacillus, Propionibacterium in a single bottle usually represent contamination (target < 3%). Adjuncts: procalcitonin, lactate, CRP. Management: early broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour, source control, fluids, vasopressors.",
[["Blood culture collection",["Before antibiotics","Chlorhexidine-alcohol skin antisepsis, allow to dry","2–3 sets from separate sites","8–10 mL per bottle (adults); 1–3 mL (children)","Aerobic + anaerobic bottles; transport immediately, do not refrigerate"]],
["Interpretation",["Pathogens (S. aureus, E. coli, Candida) — always significant","Same organism in ≥ 2 sets — significant","CoNS/diphtheroids in 1 of 2 sets — likely contaminant","Time to positivity guides line infection"]]],
{c:"Correctly collected blood cultures are the cornerstone of sepsis diagnosis, guiding de-escalation of empirical antibiotics."});

Z("micro10",5,"m",["Respiratory tract infections range from upper (common cold, pharyngitis, sinusitis, otitis) to lower (bronchitis, pneumonia, TB); most upper infections are viral, while pneumonia has bacterial, viral and fungal causes.","The airway is a long corridor: viruses usually stop at the entrance (nose, throat); serious bacteria go deeper into the lungs.",["Upper RTI: rhinovirus, coronaviruses, adenovirus, influenza; bacterial pharyngitis — S. pyogenes; diphtheria; otitis/sinusitis — pneumococcus, H. influenzae, Moraxella.","Community-acquired pneumonia: S. pneumoniae (commonest), H. influenzae, S. aureus (post-influenza), Klebsiella; atypicals — Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella; viruses — influenza, RSV, SARS-CoV-2.","Hospital/ventilator pneumonia: Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella, MRSA.","Lab: good-quality sputum (> 25 PMN, < 10 epithelial cells/LPF), Gram stain, culture, AFB/CBNAAT, urinary antigens (pneumococcus, Legionella), blood culture, BAL, multiplex PCR, serology."],"CAP top cause: S. pneumoniae; atypicals 'MCL': Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella."],
"Respiratory tract infections are the commonest infections. Upper respiratory infections: common cold (rhinoviruses, coronaviruses, RSV, adenoviruses), pharyngitis/tonsillitis (mostly viral; bacterial — Streptococcus pyogenes, C. diphtheriae, N. gonorrhoeae, Fusobacterium/Vincent angina, EBV), sinusitis and otitis media (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis), epiglottitis (Hib), laryngotracheobronchitis (croup — parainfluenza), whooping cough (Bordetella pertussis). Lower respiratory infections: bronchitis (viral), bronchiolitis in infants (RSV), pneumonia — community-acquired (S. pneumoniae most common; H. influenzae, S. aureus, Klebsiella in alcoholics/diabetics; atypical — Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, Legionella pneumophila; viruses — influenza, RSV, SARS-CoV-2, hMPV; Burkholderia pseudomallei — melioidosis in India), hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated (Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella, MRSA), aspiration pneumonia and lung abscess (anaerobes), tuberculosis, fungal (Aspergillus, PCP, histoplasmosis, mucormycosis) and parasitic (Loeffler syndrome, paragonimiasis, hydatid). Laboratory diagnosis: specimens — throat swab, nasopharyngeal swab/aspirate (viruses, pertussis), sputum (expectorated early morning, induced), endotracheal aspirate, BAL, pleural fluid, blood; sputum quality assessment by Bartlett/Murray–Washington grading (> 25 neutrophils and < 10 squamous cells per low-power field = acceptable); Gram stain, culture on blood, chocolate, MacConkey agar (semi-quantitative/quantitative for BAL: ≥ 10⁴ CFU/mL); ZN stain, CBNAAT, MGIT for TB; urinary antigen tests for pneumococcus and Legionella; blood cultures; rapid antigen tests (S. pyogenes, influenza, RSV); RT-PCR and multiplex respiratory panels for viruses and atypicals; serology (Mycoplasma IgM, cold agglutinins); KOH/calcofluor, galactomannan for fungi.",
[["Agents",[["Infection","Common agents"],["Common cold","Rhinovirus, coronavirus"],["Pharyngitis","Viruses; S. pyogenes"],["Otitis/sinusitis","Pneumococcus, H. influenzae, Moraxella"],["Croup","Parainfluenza"],["Bronchiolitis","RSV"],["CAP","Pneumococcus, H. influenzae, atypicals, influenza"],["HAP/VAP","Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella, MRSA"],["Chronic","M. tuberculosis, fungi"]]],
["Sputum quality (acceptable)","> 25 neutrophils and < 10 squamous epithelial cells per low-power field; otherwise recollect."]],
{c:"Respiratory infections are diagnosed by appropriate specimens, quality-checked sputum, culture, rapid antigen and molecular tests guided by the clinical syndrome."});

Z("micro10",6,"m",["Infectious diarrhoea and food poisoning are caused by bacteria, viruses and parasites through contaminated food and water; they may be watery (toxin/secretory) or bloody (invasive dysentery).","Some germs act like a tap left open (watery diarrhoea from toxins); others break through the gut wall (bloody dysentery).",["Watery: V. cholerae, ETEC, rotavirus, norovirus, Giardia, Cryptosporidium; bloody: Shigella, EHEC, Campylobacter, Salmonella, E. histolytica.","Food poisoning by preformed toxins (short incubation 1–6 h): S. aureus (vomiting), Bacillus cereus emetic (fried rice); 8–16 h: C. perfringens, B. cereus diarrhoeal.","Infective food poisoning (12–72 h): Salmonella, Campylobacter, V. parahaemolyticus (seafood); botulism (paralysis).","Lab: stool microscopy (pus cells, RBCs, ova/cysts, hanging drop), culture (MacConkey, DCA, XLD, TCBS), antigen tests (rotavirus), PCR panels, toxin tests (C. difficile)."],"Short incubation (1–6 h) = preformed toxin: S. aureus & B. cereus."],
"Diarrhoea is passage of three or more loose stools per day; acute infectious diarrhoea is a major cause of child mortality. Mechanisms: secretory (toxin-mediated, watery — V. cholerae, ETEC, B. cereus, S. aureus), inflammatory/invasive (bloody, with pus cells — Shigella, EIEC, Salmonella, Campylobacter jejuni, Yersinia, EHEC, C. difficile, E. histolytica), osmotic/malabsorptive (rotavirus, Giardia). Viral: rotavirus (commonest in infants; vaccine in UIP), norovirus (outbreaks, all ages), enteric adenovirus 40/41, astrovirus. Bacterial: V. cholerae, ETEC (travellers'), EPEC, EHEC O157 (HUS), Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter, V. parahaemolyticus (seafood), Aeromonas, C. difficile (antibiotic-associated, pseudomembranous colitis). Parasitic: Giardia, E. histolytica, Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Cystoisospora (AIDS). Food poisoning: illness from ingestion of food contaminated with microbes or their toxins. Intoxication by preformed toxin (short incubation): S. aureus enterotoxin (1–6 h; vomiting, cramps; dairy, meat), B. cereus emetic toxin (1–6 h; fried rice), C. botulinum (12–36 h; paralysis); toxins produced in gut: C. perfringens type A (8–16 h; diarrhoea; reheated meat), B. cereus diarrhoeal (8–16 h); infection: Salmonella (12–48 h; fever, diarrhoea; poultry, eggs), Campylobacter, V. parahaemolyticus, Listeria, E. coli O157, norovirus. Laboratory diagnosis: stool collected before antibiotics, fresh or in transport medium (Cary–Blair, VR medium, buffered glycerol saline); macroscopy; microscopy — wet mount for pus cells, RBCs, trophozoites, cysts, ova, hanging drop for Vibrio darting motility; Gram stain (Campylobacter seagull forms); culture on MacConkey, DCA, XLD, SS agar, selenite F enrichment (Salmonella, Shigella), TCBS with alkaline peptone water (Vibrio), Skirrow's/CCDA at 42 °C microaerophilic (Campylobacter), sorbitol MacConkey (O157); antigen detection (rotavirus ELISA/ICT, Giardia, Cryptosporidium), toxin assays (C. difficile GDH + toxin A/B, PCR), multiplex GI PCR panels; examination of suspected food and vomitus in outbreaks. Management: ORS and zinc; antibiotics only for dysentery, cholera, severe cases.",
[["Food poisoning",[["Incubation","Agent","Food","Features"],["1–6 h","S. aureus","Dairy, custards, meat","Vomiting"],["1–6 h","B. cereus (emetic)","Fried rice","Vomiting"],["8–16 h","C. perfringens, B. cereus (diarrhoeal)","Meat, gravy","Diarrhoea, cramps"],["12–48 h","Salmonella","Poultry, eggs","Fever, diarrhoea"],["12–36 h","C. botulinum","Canned food","Paralysis"],["24–48 h","Norovirus","Shellfish, salads","Vomiting, diarrhoea"]]],
["Watery vs bloody diarrhoea",[["Watery","Bloody"],["V. cholerae, ETEC","Shigella, EIEC"],["Rotavirus, norovirus","Campylobacter, Salmonella"],["Giardia, Cryptosporidium","EHEC, E. histolytica, C. difficile"]]]],
{c:"The clinical syndrome and incubation period point to the likely agent; stool microscopy, culture, antigen and molecular tests confirm it, while rehydration remains the core treatment."});

Z("micro10",7,"m",["Pyrexia of unknown origin (PUO) is fever > 38.3 °C on several occasions for more than 3 weeks with no diagnosis after initial investigations; infections are the commonest cause in India.","A detective case where the obvious suspects have been cleared; a systematic search of infections, cancers and inflammatory diseases is needed.",["Classic PUO: > 3 weeks, > 38.3 °C, undiagnosed after 3 days in hospital or 3 outpatient visits (Petersdorf–Beeson, modified).","Categories: infections (TB — commonest in India, enteric fever, endocarditis, abscesses, malaria, kala-azar, brucellosis, HIV, rickettsia), malignancy (lymphoma, leukaemia, RCC), connective tissue disease (Still's, SLE, vasculitis), miscellaneous (drug fever, factitious).","Microbiology: repeated blood cultures (incl. prolonged incubation), urine culture, AFB/CBNAAT, serology (Widal, Brucella, scrub typhus IgM, HIV, dengue), marrow aspirate and culture, smear for malaria/LD bodies.","Also: imaging (USG, CT, PET-CT), biopsies, echocardiography."],"PUO causes 'I-N-C-M': Infections, Neoplasms, Collagen-vascular, Miscellaneous."],
"Pyrexia of unknown origin (classic definition, Petersdorf and Beeson, modified by Durack and Street) is temperature > 38.3 °C (101 °F) on several occasions over more than 3 weeks with no diagnosis despite 3 days of hospital investigation or 3 outpatient visits. Categories: classic PUO, nosocomial PUO, neutropenic PUO and HIV-associated PUO. Causes: infections (30–40% in developing countries) — tuberculosis (especially extrapulmonary and miliary), enteric fever, infective endocarditis (culture-negative — HACEK, Bartonella, Coxiella, Brucella), intra-abdominal and hepatic abscesses, osteomyelitis, UTI/pyelonephritis, malaria, visceral leishmaniasis, brucellosis, scrub typhus and other rickettsioses, leptospirosis, HIV and opportunistic infections, CMV, EBV, fungal infections (histoplasmosis), sinusitis, dental abscess; malignancies — lymphomas, leukaemias, renal cell carcinoma, hepatoma, metastases; non-infectious inflammatory diseases — adult-onset Still disease, SLE, polymyalgia rheumatica/giant cell arteritis, vasculitis, sarcoidosis, IBD; miscellaneous — drug fever, factitious fever, thyroiditis, pulmonary emboli, haematoma, familial Mediterranean fever; undiagnosed (10–30%). Microbiological investigations: repeated blood cultures (three sets, prolonged incubation for fastidious organisms, special media for Brucella), urine culture, sputum AFB and CBNAAT, peripheral smear for malaria, rK39, serological tests (Widal, Brucella agglutination, Weil–Felix/scrub IgM ELISA, leptospira IgM, dengue, HIV, EBV, CMV, toxoplasma, ASO, Q fever), bone marrow aspirate, biopsy and culture (TB, Leishmania, Brucella, Salmonella, fungi, lymphoma), liver and lymph node biopsy with culture and histology, CSF analysis, PCR assays; along with CBC, ESR/CRP, LFT, autoantibodies, imaging (ultrasound, CT, echocardiography, PET-CT).",
[["Causes",[["Category","Examples"],["Infections","TB, enteric fever, endocarditis, abscesses, malaria, kala-azar, brucellosis, rickettsial, HIV"],["Neoplasms","Lymphoma, leukaemia, RCC, hepatoma"],["Connective tissue","Still disease, SLE, vasculitis, PMR"],["Miscellaneous","Drug fever, factitious fever, thyroiditis, PE"]]],
["Microbiological workup",["Repeated blood cultures (prolonged incubation)","Urine and sputum culture; AFB, CBNAAT","Malaria smear, rK39","Serology: Widal, Brucella, scrub typhus IgM, leptospira, HIV","Bone marrow aspirate/biopsy with culture","Tissue biopsy culture + histology"]]],
{c:"PUO requires a systematic, staged approach; in India, tuberculosis and other infections are leading causes and marrow or tissue culture often provides the answer."});

Z("micro10",8,"m",["Skin and soft tissue infections range from superficial (impetigo, folliculitis) to deep and life-threatening (cellulitis, necrotising fasciitis, gas gangrene), mostly caused by S. aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes.","Infection can sit on the surface like a stain (impetigo), spread under the skin like ink in a blotting paper (cellulitis), or race along the muscle sheath like a fire (necrotising fasciitis).",["Impetigo: S. aureus, S. pyogenes (honey-crusted); folliculitis, furuncle, carbuncle: S. aureus.","Erysipelas (raised, sharply demarcated): S. pyogenes; cellulitis: S. pyogenes, S. aureus.","Necrotising fasciitis: type I polymicrobial, type II S. pyogenes (flesh-eating); gas gangrene: C. perfringens (crepitus).","Others: diabetic foot (polymicrobial), burns (Pseudomonas), bites (Pasteurella), leprosy, cutaneous TB, mycetoma, fungal. Lab: pus/tissue Gram stain, aerobic/anaerobic culture, AST."],"Gas gangrene = 'Clostridium perfringens: crepitus, Nagler +'."],
"Skin and soft tissue infections (SSTIs) involve the epidermis, dermis, subcutaneous tissue, fascia and muscle. Superficial: impetigo (contagious; non-bullous — S. aureus, S. pyogenes, honey-coloured crusts; bullous — S. aureus exfoliative toxin), ecthyma, folliculitis (S. aureus; hot-tub folliculitis — Pseudomonas), furuncle and carbuncle (S. aureus; CA-MRSA with PVL), erythrasma (Corynebacterium minutissimum; coral-red fluorescence), staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome. Dermal/subcutaneous: erysipelas (S. pyogenes; raised, well-demarcated, bright red, face/legs), cellulitis (S. pyogenes, S. aureus; diffuse, poorly demarcated), lymphangitis, abscesses. Deep/necrotising: necrotising fasciitis — type I polymicrobial (anaerobes + Enterobacteriaceae; diabetics, post-surgery; Fournier gangrene of perineum), type II monomicrobial (S. pyogenes, 'flesh-eating', with toxic shock; also MRSA), type III (Vibrio vulnificus, Aeromonas — water); clostridial myonecrosis (gas gangrene — C. perfringens α-toxin; traumatic wounds; crepitus, bronze discoloration, shock) and non-clostridial myositis; pyomyositis (S. aureus; tropical). Special settings: diabetic foot (polymicrobial, osteomyelitis), burns (Pseudomonas, S. aureus, Acinetobacter, Candida), surgical site infections, animal bites (Pasteurella multocida, Capnocytophaga), human bites (Eikenella, anaerobes), water exposure (Aeromonas, M. marinum), traumatic implantation (mycetoma, sporotrichosis, chromoblastomycosis); viral (HSV, VZV, warts, molluscum), mycobacterial (leprosy, cutaneous TB), fungal (dermatophytes, candidiasis). Laboratory diagnosis: pus aspirate or tissue biopsy (better than swabs); Gram stain (e.g., Gram-positive cocci in clusters/chains; large Gram-positive bacilli without spores and few pus cells in gas gangrene); aerobic and anaerobic culture; identification and AST (MRSA detection); blood cultures in systemic illness; special stains and cultures (AFB, fungal); Nagler reaction for C. perfringens; imaging (gas, fascial involvement). Management: drainage, debridement (urgent for necrotising infections), appropriate antibiotics.",
[["Classification",[["Depth","Infection","Main agents"],["Epidermis","Impetigo","S. aureus, S. pyogenes"],["Hair follicle","Folliculitis, furuncle, carbuncle","S. aureus"],["Upper dermis","Erysipelas","S. pyogenes"],["Dermis + subcutaneous","Cellulitis","S. pyogenes, S. aureus"],["Fascia","Necrotising fasciitis","Polymicrobial; S. pyogenes"],["Muscle","Gas gangrene, pyomyositis","C. perfringens; S. aureus"]]]],
{c:"SSTIs vary from trivial to fatal; recognising depth and setting predicts the agent, and pus or tissue culture with urgent surgery for necrotising infections is essential."});

Z("micro10",9,"m",["Congenital infections are acquired by the fetus during pregnancy (TORCH), while neonatal infections are acquired during or after birth (sepsis, meningitis, ophthalmia neonatorum, tetanus).","Infections can reach the baby through the placenta (before birth), the birth canal (during birth) or the environment and caregivers (after birth).",["Congenital (transplacental): Toxoplasma, rubella, CMV, HSV, syphilis, HIV, VZV, parvovirus B19, Zika, HBV.","Perinatal/neonatal: GBS, E. coli K1, Listeria, Klebsiella (sepsis, meningitis); gonococcus, Chlamydia (ophthalmia neonatorum); HSV, HBV, HIV; C. tetani (neonatal tetanus).","Early-onset sepsis (< 72 h) from maternal flora; late-onset (> 72 h) from hospital (Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, CoNS, Candida).","Lab: neonatal IgM (TORCH), PCR, blood culture, CSF, conjunctival smear Gram/Giemsa; sepsis screen (CRP, TLC, micro-ESR)."],"Ophthalmia neonatorum: gonococcus (day 2–5), Chlamydia (day 5–14)."],
"Congenital infections are transmitted from mother to fetus in utero, mostly transplacentally: TORCH agents (Toxoplasma gondii; Others — syphilis, VZV, parvovirus B19, HIV, Listeria, Zika, enteroviruses; Rubella; Cytomegalovirus — commonest; Herpes simplex — mainly intrapartum), causing abortion, stillbirth, IUGR, malformations (rubella — cataract, PDA, deafness; Zika — microcephaly), hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, chorioretinitis, intracranial calcification and later sequelae (deafness, developmental delay). Perinatal infections are acquired during passage through the birth canal: group B streptococcus, E. coli, Listeria, HSV, HBV, HIV, gonococcus, Chlamydia, Candida. Neonatal (postnatal) infections: neonatal sepsis — early-onset (< 72 hours; maternal genital flora — GBS, E. coli, Listeria, Klebsiella; risk factors — prolonged rupture of membranes, chorioamnionitis, prematurity) and late-onset (> 72 hours; nosocomial — Klebsiella pneumoniae, Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, S. aureus, CoNS, Candida; in India Gram-negatives with high resistance); neonatal meningitis (GBS, E. coli K1, Listeria); ophthalmia neonatorum (conjunctivitis in first 28 days — N. gonorrhoeae at 2–5 days, purulent, risk of corneal perforation; C. trachomatis at 5–14 days; also S. aureus, chemical); neonatal tetanus (umbilical stump contamination); umbilical sepsis (omphalitis); necrotising enterocolitis; diarrhoea. Laboratory diagnosis: congenital — neonatal specific IgM (does not cross placenta), persistent or rising IgG beyond 6–12 months, PCR (urine/saliva for CMV within 3 weeks, amniotic fluid), virus isolation, dark-ground and VDRL/TPHA (syphilis), placental histology; neonatal sepsis — blood culture (gold standard; 1 mL in paediatric bottle), CSF analysis and culture, urine culture (late-onset), sepsis screen (TLC < 5000, ANC, band:total ratio > 0.2, CRP > 1 mg/dL, micro-ESR, procalcitonin); ophthalmia — conjunctival scraping Gram stain (intracellular Gram-negative diplococci), Giemsa (inclusions), culture, NAAT. Prevention: antenatal screening (syphilis, HIV, HBsAg), rubella vaccination, intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis for GBS, PPTCT, birth-dose HBV vaccine with HBIG, ocular prophylaxis, clean delivery and cord care, TT/Td in pregnancy, breastfeeding, hand hygiene.",
[["Routes and agents",[["Route","Agents"],["Transplacental","Toxoplasma, rubella, CMV, syphilis, HIV, VZV, parvovirus, Zika"],["Intrapartum","GBS, E. coli, HSV, HBV, HIV, gonococcus, Chlamydia"],["Postnatal","Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, S. aureus, CoNS, Candida, C. tetani"]]],
["Neonatal sepsis",[["Type","Onset","Agents"],["Early-onset","< 72 h","GBS, E. coli, Listeria, Klebsiella"],["Late-onset","> 72 h","Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, S. aureus, CoNS, Candida"]]]],
{c:"Congenital and neonatal infections are major contributors to infant morbidity; antenatal screening, safe delivery and infection control prevent many, while neonatal IgM, PCR and blood culture guide diagnosis."});

Z("micro10",10,"m",["Eye infections affect the conjunctiva (conjunctivitis), cornea (keratitis), inner eye (endophthalmitis) and eyelids; causes include bacteria, viruses, fungi, Chlamydia and Acanthamoeba.","Each part of the eye has its usual intruders: viruses and bacteria for the conjunctiva, fungi after vegetable injury for the cornea, contact lenses inviting Pseudomonas and Acanthamoeba.",["Conjunctivitis: adenovirus (epidemic keratoconjunctivitis), enterovirus 70/coxsackie A24 (acute haemorrhagic), S. aureus, pneumococcus, H. influenzae (pink eye), gonococcus, Chlamydia (trachoma, inclusion conjunctivitis).","Keratitis: bacterial (Pseudomonas — contact lens, pneumococcus), fungal (Fusarium, Aspergillus after vegetable trauma — common in India), viral (HSV dendritic ulcer), Acanthamoeba (contact lens, severe pain).","Endophthalmitis: post-operative (CoNS), endogenous (Candida); retinitis: CMV (AIDS), Toxoplasma.","Lab: conjunctival swab, corneal scraping — Gram, KOH/calcofluor, Giemsa; culture on blood, chocolate, SDA, non-nutrient agar with E. coli (Acanthamoeba); PCR."],"Corneal ulcer after leaf injury = fungus; contact lens = Pseudomonas or Acanthamoeba."],
"Infections of the eye and adnexa: blepharitis, stye (hordeolum — S. aureus), dacryocystitis (pneumococcus, S. aureus); conjunctivitis — bacterial (S. aureus, S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae biogroup aegyptius — Koch–Weeks bacillus, Moraxella lacunata — angular, N. gonorrhoeae — hyperacute purulent), viral (adenovirus types 8, 19, 37 — epidemic keratoconjunctivitis; types 3, 7 — pharyngoconjunctival fever; enterovirus 70 and coxsackievirus A24 — acute haemorrhagic conjunctivitis; HSV, VZV, measles), chlamydial (trachoma — C. trachomatis A–C, leading infectious cause of blindness; adult inclusion conjunctivitis — D–K), allergic; ophthalmia neonatorum; keratitis (corneal ulcer) — bacterial (Pseudomonas aeruginosa with contact lenses — rapid perforation; S. pneumoniae — ulcus serpens; S. aureus; Moraxella; Nocardia), fungal (Fusarium, Aspergillus flavus, dematiaceous fungi; after trauma with vegetable matter in agricultural workers; feathery margins, satellite lesions, hypopyon — very common in India), viral (HSV — dendritic ulcer, stromal keratitis; VZV — herpes zoster ophthalmicus), parasitic (Acanthamoeba — contact lens wearers, ring infiltrate, severe pain; Microsporidia); endophthalmitis — exogenous post-operative (CoNS, S. aureus, streptococci, Pseudomonas) or post-traumatic (Bacillus cereus), endogenous (Candida, Klebsiella liver abscess); orbital cellulitis (S. aureus, streptococci, H. influenzae, mucormycosis); chorioretinitis/retinitis — CMV in AIDS, Toxoplasma, toxocariasis, syphilis, TB, onchocerciasis (river blindness). Laboratory diagnosis: specimens — conjunctival swabs, corneal scrapings (with Kimura spatula, inoculated directly onto media), aqueous and vitreous taps; Gram stain, Giemsa (inclusions, multinucleate cells), KOH/calcofluor white (fungal hyphae, Acanthamoeba cysts), modified ZN (Nocardia); culture on blood agar, chocolate agar, thioglycollate broth, SDA, non-nutrient agar overlaid with E. coli (Acanthamoeba — trophozoite tracks); viral culture/PCR (adenovirus, HSV, CMV), NAAT for Chlamydia and gonococcus; confocal microscopy for fungi and Acanthamoeba. Prevention: hygiene, contact lens care, SAFE strategy for trachoma, neonatal ocular prophylaxis.",
[["Infections and agents",[["Infection","Common agents"],["Bacterial conjunctivitis","S. aureus, pneumococcus, H. influenzae"],["Hyperacute conjunctivitis","N. gonorrhoeae"],["Viral conjunctivitis","Adenovirus, enterovirus 70, coxsackie A24"],["Trachoma","C. trachomatis A–C"],["Bacterial keratitis","Pseudomonas, pneumococcus, S. aureus"],["Fungal keratitis","Fusarium, Aspergillus"],["Viral keratitis","HSV (dendritic)"],["Acanthamoeba keratitis","Contact lens wearers"],["Endophthalmitis","CoNS, B. cereus, Candida"],["Retinitis","CMV, Toxoplasma"]]]],
{c:"Eye infections can rapidly threaten vision; corneal scrapings examined by Gram, KOH and appropriate cultures guide urgent targeted therapy."});
